  </section>
</main>

<footer>
  <div class="wrap">
    Cozyology Blog Library · Internal writing resource for our content team
    <div class="tagrow"><span>Warm</span><span>Helpful</span><span>Honest</span><span>Original</span></div>
  </div>
</footer>

<script>
  const DATA = {
    /* ============ CURTAIN KNOW-HOW ============ */
    know: {
      eyebrow: "Category · Practical",
      title: "Curtain Know-How",
      intro: "The posts people search for when they have a specific problem — how to measure, how to hang, how to clean, what to buy. They earn trust by being genuinely useful.",
      who: ["Someone shopping and unsure what to pick", "A first-time buyer worried about measuring wrong", "A reader who just wants a clear, no-fuss answer"],
      goal: ["Answer one clear question completely", "Leave the reader confident to act", "Show Cozyology knows curtains inside out"],
      structures: [
        {
          pick: "Structure A", name: "Answer First", when: "Best when readers want a quick, direct answer.",
          flow: ["The question", "Short answer", "The details / steps", "One expert tip", "Gentle next step"]
        },
        {
          pick: "Structure B", name: "Fix the Mistake", when: "Great for topics where people commonly go wrong.",
          flow: ["A common mistake", "Why it happens", "How to do it right", "Quick recap"]
        },
        {
          pick: "Structure C", name: "Compare & Decide", when: "Use when there are options to weigh.",
          flow: ["The choice to make", "Option by option", "Pros & cons", "Your recommendation"]
        }
      ],
      mine: ["Open with a question a friend actually asked you, in your own words.", "Add one real detail — a window in your own home, a number you measured.", "Change how many steps or options you include; there's no fixed count.", "Pick a different structure than the samples below so your post reads fresh."],
      tips: ["Keep sentences short — if one feels long when read aloud, split it.", "Use real numbers and inches; specifics feel trustworthy.", "500–800 words is plenty for a practical post.", "Note where a photo or simple diagram would help the reader."],
      samples: [
        {
          stag: "Uses Structure A · Answer First", meta: "Sample 1 · ~640 words",
          title: "How to Measure Your Windows for Curtains (Step by Step)",
          body: `
      <p><b>The quick answer:</b> measure your rod, not your window, then decide how far down you want the fabric to fall — and add a bit of extra width so the curtains look full rather than flat. Here's the full method.</p>
      <h4>What to grab first</h4>
      <ul><li>A metal tape measure (cloth ones stretch and mislead you)</li><li>Somewhere to jot numbers — paper or your phone</li><li>About two minutes for each window</li></ul>
      <h4>Step 1 · Find your width</h4>
      <p>Run the tape along your rod from one end to the other, skipping the decorative caps. That figure is your starting width. For a soft, gathered finish, plan for roughly one and a half to two times that number in curtain fabric — so a 50-inch rod wears 75 to 100 inches of curtain beautifully.</p>
      <h4>Step 2 · Choose your drop</h4>
      <p>Measure from the rod down to where you'd like the fabric to end. Three heights are popular: stopping near the sill (handy in kitchens and bathrooms), reaching the floor (the go-to for living rooms and bedrooms), or spilling slightly onto the floor for a soft, romantic pool of fabric.</p>
      <h4>Step 3 · Confirm before you order</h4>
      <p>Record width and drop for each window separately — even windows in one room aren't always twins. If a number falls between sizes, size up. Curtains a touch long look deliberate; curtains a touch short look like an accident.</p>
      <h4>The mistake worth avoiding</h4>
      <p>Plenty of people measure the glass instead of the rod. Curtains should stretch a little past the frame on both sides, so light doesn't sneak in and the window reads as bigger. Set your rod wider than the frame and the whole room feels taller and lighter at once.</p>
      <p>With your numbers in hand, you're ready to pick a style you'll love.</p>
      <span class="note">🔁 Swap-in spot: close with a link to a Cozyology collection.</span>`},
        {
          stag: "Uses Structure C · Compare & Decide", meta: "Sample 2 · ~560 words",
          title: "Blackout, Sheer, or In Between? How to Pick Your Light Level",
          body: `
      <p>Before colour or pattern, one choice shapes how a curtain actually works in your room: how much light do you want it to let through? Here's how the main options compare, so you can decide quickly.</p>
      <h4>Sheer — soft, glowing light</h4>
      <p>Sheers filter daylight into a gentle wash and keep a little daytime privacy, while still feeling open and airy. The trade-off: they don't darken a room or block a streetlight at night, so they shine best where mood matters more than total privacy.</p>
      <h4>Light-filtering — the middle ground</h4>
      <p>These soften the light and offer more privacy than sheers without shutting the day out entirely. They're a flexible, everyday choice for living rooms and spaces you use at all hours. The trade-off is they won't give you true darkness for sleeping in.</p>
      <h4>Blackout — real darkness</h4>
      <p>Blackout fabric blocks nearly all light, which makes it the friend of good sleep, nurseries, and media rooms. It also helps with warmth and noise. The trade-off: on their own they can feel heavy by day, which is why many people layer them with sheers.</p>
      <h4>So which should you choose?</h4>
      <p>Match the fabric to the job of the room. Bedroom or baby's room? Lean blackout. A bright living room you want to feel calm and airy? Sheers. Not sure, or the room does a bit of everything? Light-filtering is the safe pick — or layer two, and get the best of both.</p>
      <span class="note">🔁 Swap-in spot: link to sheer, light-filtering, and blackout collections.</span>`}
      ],
      outlines: [
        { badge: "Angle", title: "How High Should You Hang Curtains?", pts: ["The simple 'high and wide' rule", "Typical heights for standard ceilings", "How height changes a room's feel", "One tip to make ceilings look taller"] },
        { badge: "Angle", title: "How to Wash and Care for Your Curtains", pts: ["Read the fabric label first", "Gentle wash vs. dry clean", "Keeping linen and velvet fresh", "How often you truly need to wash"] }
      ]
    },

    /* ============ DESIGN INSPIRATION ============ */
    design: {
      eyebrow: "Category · Style",
      title: "Design Inspiration",
      intro: "Posts about the look and feeling of a space. Readers come for ideas and mood, not instructions — so help them picture the result and fall for a style.",
      who: ["Someone redecorating and hunting for inspiration", "A reader chasing a specific aesthetic (modern, natural, airy)", "A person who buys with their eyes and their emotions"],
      goal: ["Paint a picture they can imagine at home", "Tie a style to a feeling (calm, warm, elegant)", "Show Cozyology has taste, not just stock"],
      structures: [
        {
          pick: "Structure A", name: "Scene & Feeling", when: "Best for evoking a mood around one style.",
          flow: ["A feeling or scene", "Describe the look", "3–5 ways to use it", "Who it suits", "Warm close"]
        },
        {
          pick: "Structure B", name: "Trend Explainer", when: "Good for naming and unpacking a specific style.",
          flow: ["Name the style", "Why people love it", "Its key features", "One easy way to try it"]
