          body: `
      <p>Designers make choosing curtains look effortless, but they're really just following a few quiet principles. Learn them, and you can shop with the same calm confidence. Here's how the pros think it through.</p>
      <h4>Function comes before fabric</h4>
      <p>Before a single colour is considered, a designer asks what the curtain has to do. Darken a room for sleep? Tame harsh sun? Add privacy? Hold in warmth? The room's job settles everything that follows — so settle yours first.</p>
      <h4>Length isn't up for debate</h4>
      <p>Pros nearly always run curtains right to the floor. It's the one detail that separates a finished room from a half-done one. They also set the rod high and wide to give windows presence. If you borrow a single habit, borrow this.</p>
      <h4>Fabric is judged by weight and drape</h4>
      <p>A good curtain has some heft — it hangs and folds smoothly instead of looking thin or clinging to the window. Natural fibres like linen and cotton tend to drape more gracefully and last longer than the cheapest synthetics. Whenever you can, feel the fabric; your hands notice quality fast.</p>
      <h4>Colour is decided last</h4>
      <p>With function and fabric handled, colour becomes the enjoyable part. Designers often pick a shade a little softer or deeper than the wall for a layered, restful effect rather than an exact match. Neutrals stay timeless; a colour adds character.</p>
      <h4>A checklist you can borrow</h4>
      <ul><li>Decide the curtain's job first</li><li>Hang high, wide, and to the floor</li><li>Choose fabric with weight and good drape</li><li>Pick colour last, and lean timeless</li></ul>
      <p>Follow those four and you'll make choices a professional would quietly approve of — no training needed.</p>
      <span class="note">🔁 Swap-in spot: link to a curated 'designer favourites' collection.</span>`},
