          body: `
      <p>Two curtains can look almost identical online and feel worlds apart in your hands. Here's what separates a well-made curtain from a flimsy one — so you can tell the difference before you buy.</p>
      <h4>Weight and drape</h4>
      <p>Quality fabric has a little heft and falls into smooth, even folds. Thin, weightless fabric tends to look papery and cling to the window. If a curtain hangs in soft vertical lines, that's a good sign; if it looks flat and stiff, be cautious.</p>
      <h4>Hems and stitching</h4>
      <p>Turn to the edges. A well-made curtain has a generous, weighted bottom hem and straight, even stitching with no loose threads. A skimpy hem and wandering stitches are the first places corners get cut.</p>
      <h4>The header and lining</h4>
      <p>The top of the curtain should gather or pleat neatly and hold its shape. A lining is a quiet mark of quality — it helps the curtain hang better, block more light, and last longer. Unlined bargain curtains often fade and sag sooner.</p>
      <h4>The honest trade-off</h4>
      <p>Well-made curtains cost more up front, and that's fair to weigh. But a cheap set you replace every couple of years often costs more in the end — and never looks quite right in between. Buying well usually means buying once.</p>
      <h4>Your quick checklist</h4>
      <ul><li>Does it have weight and fall into smooth folds?</li><li>Are the hems full and the stitching straight?</li><li>Does the header hold its shape?</li><li>Is it lined?</li></ul>
      <span class="note">🔁 Swap-in spot: link to lined or made-to-measure styles.</span>`}
